{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 2378 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "2e9e23f1c0b2e8ae70e152b154a9fa5481726acfad7646351e86453fe6cf9218" :edb/db-before-t 2377 :edb/db-after-t 2378 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535690 50 #inst "2026-10-09T05:14:07.811Z" 13194139535690 true] [17592186100910 1020 #edb/ref 17592186361544 13194139535690 true] [17592186100911 1020 #edb/ref 17592186361523 13194139535690 true] [17592186100911 1020 #edb/ref 17592186361530 13194139535690 true] [17592186100911 1020 #edb/ref 17592186361537 13194139535690 true] [17592186100912 1020 #edb/ref 17592186361516 13194139535690 true] [17592186361516 1017 #edb/ref 17592186361517 13194139535690 true] [17592186361516 1089 "q-134529" 13194139535690 true] [17592186361516 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535690 true] [17592186361516 1100 "Calculate the percentage error obtained when approximating the value of $(0.9)^{6}$ using the first three terms of the binomial expansion of $(1+x)^{6}$ in ascending powers of $x.$ Round your final percentage error to two decimal places." 13194139535690 true] [17592186361516 1113 #uuid "61a16f5c-99a6-5b86-b6ca-12e8e6087eb0" 13194139535690 true] [17592186361516 1145 "Recall that when $n$ is a positive integer, \n\n$$\n(1+x)^{n}=1+nx+\\frac{1}{2!}n(n-1){x}^{2}+⋯+{x}^{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(1+x)}^{6} & =1+6x+\\frac{1}{2!}\\cdot 6\\cdot 5\\cdot {x}^{2}+⋯+{x}^{n} \\\\  & =1+6x+15{x}^{2}+⋯+{x}^{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for small $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n{(1+x)}^{6}≈1+6x+15{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute $x=-0.1$ into the above approximation of $(1+x)^{6}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(0.9)^{6} & =(1+(-0.1))^{6} \\\\  & ≈1+6(-0.1)+15(-0.1)^{2} \\\\  & =1-6(0.1)+15(0.01) \\\\  & =1-0.6+0.15 \\\\  & =0.55\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using a calculator, we can compute the percentage error, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}%\\,error & =\\frac{\\text{amount of error}}{\\text{exact value}}\\times 100 \\\\  & =\\frac{|(0.9)^{6}-0.55|}{(0.9)^{6}}\\times 100 \\\\  & ≈3.49%\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places." 13194139535690 true] [17592186361517 1000 #edb/ref 17592186361518 13194139535690 true] [17592186361517 1000 #edb/ref 17592186361519 13194139535690 true] [17592186361517 1000 #edb/ref 17592186361520 13194139535690 true] [17592186361517 1000 #edb/ref 17592186361521 13194139535690 true] [17592186361517 1000 #edb/ref 17592186361522 13194139535690 true] [17592186361517 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535690 true] [17592186361517 1041 "selection" 13194139535690 true] [17592186361517 1104 #edb/ref 17592186361519 13194139535690 true] [17592186361517 1110 #uuid "b758f262-7cd7-5727-a4fe-c622b22ab767" 13194139535690 true] [17592186361518 1034 "3.45%" 13194139535690 true] [17592186361518 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535690 true] [17592186361518 1111 #uuid "6ef46c96-0ad0-53e2-a066-4466cb3e4014" 13194139535690 true] [17592186361519 1034 "3.49%" 13194139535690 true] [17592186361519 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535690 true] [17592186361519 1111 #uuid "162ec16a-3b4d-5f4f-94ac-071a40d5c5ce" 13194139535690 true] [17592186361520 1034 "3.47%" 13194139535690 true] [17592186361520 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535690 true] [17592186361520 1111 #uuid "976b4899-7903-5cb9-82f0-93e82a108a87" 13194139535690 true] [17592186361521 1034 "3.55%" 13194139535690 true] [17592186361521 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535690 true] [17592186361521 1111 #uuid "d0756de8-e391-5c40-9840-3edde5e9fe77" 13194139535690 true] [17592186361522 1034 "3.52%" 13194139535690 true] [17592186361522 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535690 true] [17592186361522 1111 #uuid "50506732-0e79-5a81-ae44-6d25598a764a" 13194139535690 true] [17592186361523 1017 #edb/ref 17592186361524 13194139535690 true] [17592186361523 1089 "q-134648" 13194139535690 true] [17592186361523 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535690 true] [17592186361523 1100 "Use the first three terms of the binomial expansion of $(3+x)^{5}$ in ascending powers of $x$ to approximate $(2.96)^{5}.$ Give your answer to three decimal places." 13194139535690 true] [17592186361523 1113 #uuid "e3b60ef2-0e7e-5aa9-a047-53848ed93935" 13194139535690 true] [17592186361523 1145 "The binomial theorem for positive integer $n$ states that\n\n$$\n(a+b)^{n}=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{0}){a}^{n}{b}^{0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{1}){a}^{n-1}{b}^{1}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{2}){a}^{n-2}{b}^{2}+…+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{n}){a}^{0}{b}^{n}.\n$$\n\nBy substituting $a=3,$ $b=x,$ and $n=5,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(3+x)}^{5} & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{0}){(3)}^{5}{(x)}^{0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{1}){(3)}^{5-1}{(x)}^{1}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{2}){(3)}^{5-2}{(x)}^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =1{(3)}^{5}+5{(3)}^{4}{(x)}^{1}+10{(3)}^{3}{(x)}^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =243+5(81)(x)+10(27)({x}^{2})+⋯ \\\\  & =243+405x+270{x}^{2}+⋯\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for small $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n{(3+x)}^{5}≈243+405x+270{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $x=-0.04$ into the above approximation of $(3+x)^{5}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2.96)^{5} & =(3+(-0.04))^{5} \\\\  & ≈243+405(-0.04)+270(-0.04)^{2} \\\\  & =243-405(0.04)+270(0.001\\,6) \\\\  & =243-16.2+0.432 \\\\  & =227.232,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto three decimal places." 13194139535690 true] [17592186361524 1000 #edb/ref 17592186361525 13194139535690 true] [17592186361524 1000 #edb/ref 17592186361526 13194139535690 true] [17592186361524 1000 #edb/ref 17592186361527 13194139535690 true] [17592186361524 1000 #edb/ref 17592186361528 13194139535690 true] [17592186361524 1000 #edb/ref 17592186361529 13194139535690 true] [17592186361524 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535690 true] [17592186361524 1041 "selection" 13194139535690 true] [17592186361524 1104 #edb/ref 17592186361529 13194139535690 true] [17592186361524 1110 #uuid "3904a261-9f3b-50ed-9af7-4be29d6318c8" 13194139535690 true] [17592186361525 1034 "227.226" 13194139535690 true] [17592186361525 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535690 true] [17592186361525 1111 #uuid 